// `@source` says what its files are written in — C, Objective-C, C++ or
// Objective-C++ — and for C++ the standard they are compiled to: a
// language it does not know, a standard it does not know, and a standard
// given for C files are each refused.

@source("a.c", language = "fortran")
extern "C" {}

@source("b.cpp", language = "c++", standard = "c++99")
extern "C" {}

@source("a.c", language = "c", standard = "c++20")
extern "C" {}

fn main() = {}
